### What are the core entities?

The core entities are `User`, `Restaurant`, `MenuItem`, `Order`, `OrderItem`, `DeliveryAgent`, `OrderStatus`, and `PaymentStrategy`.

```java
package com.bank.delivery.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class MenuItem {
    private final String itemId;
    private final String name;
    private final BigDecimal price;

    public MenuItem(String itemId, String name, BigDecimal price) {
        this.itemId = itemId;
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public BigDecimal getPrice() { return price; }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.delivery.model;

public enum OrderStatus {
    PLACED, ACCEPTED, PREPARING, OUT_FOR_DELIVERY, DELIVERED, CANCELLED
}
```

### How does the order status state machine work?

The order status progresses through a strict sequence. Legal transitions are enforced so an order cannot jump illegally.

```java
package com.bank.delivery.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Order {
    private final String orderId;
    private final User user;
    private final Restaurant restaurant;
    private final List<OrderItem> items;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private DeliveryAgent agent;
    private final List<OrderStatusListener> listeners;

    public Order(String orderId, User user, Restaurant restaurant,
                 List<OrderItem> items) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.user = user;
        this.restaurant = restaurant;
        this.items = items;
        this.status = OrderStatus.PLACED;
        this.listeners = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void transitionTo(OrderStatus newStatus) {
        if (!isValidTransition(status, newStatus)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                    "Cannot transition from " + status
                    + " to " + newStatus);
        }
        this.status = newStatus;
        notifyListeners();
        System.out.println("Order " + orderId + " → " + newStatus);
    }

    private boolean isValidTransition(OrderStatus from,
                                       OrderStatus to) {
        return switch (from) {
            case PLACED -> to == OrderStatus.ACCEPTED
                    || to == OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
            case ACCEPTED -> to == OrderStatus.PREPARING
                    || to == OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
            case PREPARING -> to == OrderStatus.OUT_FOR_DELIVERY;
            case OUT_FOR_DELIVERY -> to == OrderStatus.DELIVERED;
            default -> false;
        };
    }

    public BigDecimal getTotal() {
        return items.stream()
                .map(i -> i.getMenuItem().getPrice()
                        .multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(i.getQuantity())))
                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }

    public void addListener(OrderStatusListener listener) {
        listeners.add(listener);
    }

    private void notifyListeners() {
        for (OrderStatusListener l : listeners) {
            l.onStatusChange(this, status);
        }
    }

    public String getOrderId() { return orderId; }
    public OrderStatus getStatus() { return status; }
    public void setAgent(DeliveryAgent agent) { this.agent = agent; }
}
```

### How does the Observer pattern decouple reactions?

When the order status changes, many systems must react: user notification, restaurant dashboard, delivery map, loyalty points. Instead of the order calling each directly, it publishes a status-change event and every observer reacts independently.

```java
package com.bank.delivery.model;

public interface OrderStatusListener {
    void onStatusChange(Order order, OrderStatus newStatus);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.delivery.listener;

import com.bank.delivery.model.*;

public class UserNotificationListener implements OrderStatusListener {
    @Override
    public void onStatusChange(Order order, OrderStatus newStatus) {
        System.out.println("Push notification: Order "
                + order.getOrderId() + " is now " + newStatus);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.delivery.listener;

import com.bank.delivery.model.*;

public class LoyaltyPointsListener implements OrderStatusListener {
    @Override
    public void onStatusChange(Order order, OrderStatus newStatus) {
        if (newStatus == OrderStatus.DELIVERED) {
            System.out.println("Credited loyalty points for order "
                    + order.getOrderId());
        }
    }
}
```

Adding a new reaction is a new listener class, not a change to the order code.

### How does payment work?

Payment goes through a strategy interface so the app supports card, UPI, and wallet interchangeably.

```java
package com.bank.delivery.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface PaymentStrategy {
    boolean pay(String userId, BigDecimal amount);
}

public class UpiPaymentStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    @Override
    public boolean pay(String userId, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("UPI payment of " + amount
                + " for user " + userId);
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How is a delivery agent assigned?

When the food is ready (status PREPARING → OUT_FOR_DELIVERY), a delivery agent is assigned by proximity to the restaurant.

```java
package com.bank.delivery.service;

import com.bank.delivery.model.DeliveryAgent;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class AgentAssigner {
    public DeliveryAgent assignNearest(List<DeliveryAgent> available,
                                        double restaurantLat,
                                        double restaurantLon) {
        return available.stream()
                .min(Comparator.comparingDouble(
                        a -> distance(a.getLat(), a.getLon(),
                                restaurantLat, restaurantLon)))
                .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException(
                        "No delivery agent available"));
    }

    private double distance(double lat1, double lon1,
                            double lat2, double lon2) {
        return Math.sqrt(Math.pow(lat2 - lat1, 2)
                + Math.pow(lon2 - lon1, 2));
    }
}
```

### How does rating work?

After delivery, ratings attach to both the restaurant and the agent.

```java
package com.bank.delivery.model;

public class Rating {
    private final String orderId;
    private final int restaurantScore; // 1-5
    private final int agentScore;      // 1-5
    private final String review;

    public Rating(String orderId, int restaurantScore,
                  int agentScore, String review) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.restaurantScore = restaurantScore;
        this.agentScore = agentScore;
        this.review = review;
    }
}
```

### What about extensibility?

The Observer pattern makes the design scale cleanly. When the product team wants to add a new reaction to order delivery, such as sending a feedback email, it is a new `OrderStatusListener` class with zero change to the order code.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Status transitions and notifications are decoupled through Observer, which is what keeps a delivery platform maintainable as features multiply.
- The payment strategy can also handle refunds on cancellation by adding a `refund()` method to the interface.
- In production, agent assignment uses geospatial indexing (e.g., Redis GEO) rather than a brute-force distance scan.
- Order status changes should be persisted in an event store for audit and replay.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you add a promotion or discount feature?
- How do you handle a delivery agent who becomes unavailable mid-delivery?
- How do you prevent a restaurant from being overwhelmed with orders?
- How would you implement real-time order tracking?
- How does the system handle payment failure after the order is placed?
